# {{.Name}}

{{if .Discord -}}
This Chatanium v2 starter replies `pong` when a non-bot user types `!ping` in
channels allowed by the runtime.
{{- else}}
This Chatanium v2 starter exercises the core lifecycle. It requests no
capabilities and needs no backend or configuration.
{{- end}}

## Build, install, and run

{{if .BuildTarget -}}
Run these commands from the Chatanium SDK checkout used by `chatanium new`.
You do not need Docker or a registry.
If `./bin/chatanium` is missing, run `make build-runtime` first.

```sh
make modules MODULE={{.BuildTarget}}
./bin/chatanium module install ./bin/{{.BuildTarget}}/module.oci.tar
./bin/chatanium serve
```
{{- if .Discord}}

Before installing, create a Discord bot, enable its privileged **Message Content
Intent**, and add it to your guild with permission to view and send messages in
the chosen text channel.

On first use, enter the Discord bot token and review the permissions. The host
looks up the bot identity and keeps the token private. Installation grants no
server or channel access.

With the host serving, run `./bin/chatanium acl set {{.Name}}` in another
terminal. Choose servers or channels by name and approve the change. Send
`!ping` in an allowed channel to check the reply.
{{- else}}

Review the installation and type `y` to approve it. The module becomes ready
when the host runs INIT. START, DRAIN, and STOP exercise the rest of the
lifecycle without making external calls.
{{- end}}
{{- else}}
{{if .Discord}}
Run `make prepare-sdk` from the Chatanium SDK checkout first.
{{- end}}

From this module's directory:

```sh
go test ./...
go build .
```

To package an OCI archive with `make modules`, put the module under the SDK
checkout (for example, in `local-modules/{{.Name}}`), update the replacements
in `go.mod`, and pass its checkout-relative path as `MODULE`.
{{- end}}

## Change the module

Edit `manifest.json` to change the module declaration. The Go executable embeds
it, and `make modules` copies it into the OCI archive. Rebuild after changing
the manifest or Go code. If the module and SDK move independently, update the
replacements in `go.mod`.
